   "text": "Read only the page below. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse. Answer each question from the page alone. When the page does not say, answer \"Not stated\".\n\n<<<PAGE\n# release-notes\n\n## What it does\n\n`release-notes` drafts release notes from merged PRs between two tags.\n\n## When to use it\n\nUse it to gather PRs and create a release-note draft. Use `technical-writing` when you need to edit prose.\n\n## How it works\n\nThe skill runs only when you invoke it manually:\n\n```text\n/release-notes <from-tag> <to-tag>\n```\n\nFor example, with illustrative tags:\n\n```text\n/release-notes v1.0.0 v1.1.0\n```\n\nYou must be signed in through `gh`. The skill stops if `gh auth status` fails or either tag does not exist.\n\nIt groups PRs by Conventional Commit type, with features, fixes, and breaking changes first. It skips PRs labeled `skip-notes`. When a PR has labels for multiple types, it uses the group named by most labels.\n\nEach breaking change needs a migration line. If the PR body has none, the draft marks it `TODO: migration`.\n\n## What you get\n\nThe skill writes `RELEASE_NOTES.md` in the working tree and stops. It never publishes.\nPAGE>>>\n\nQuestions:\n(a) Will it publish a release?\n(b) How do you run it?\n(c) What happens to a breaking change with no migration note?\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"a\": \"...\", \"b\": \"...\", \"c\": \"...\"}. Start the answer to (a) with Yes, No, or Not stated.",
